Allergic Rhinitis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Clear watery rhinorrhea, Paroxysmal sneezing, Pale boggy nasal turbinates

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-005
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, unrelenting paroxysmal sneezing and copious rhinorrhea causing respiratory distress and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to exclude early anaphylaxis, acute infectious airway compromise, or foreign body aspiration. Intervention could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the severity of perceived respiratory distress and significant caregiver anxiety.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He can't stop sneezing, his nose is completely blocked, and he is struggling to breathe through his mouth."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with paroxysmal, rapid, repetitive episodes of severe, disruptive sneezing that began abruptly this evening after playing outdoors. There is copious, entirely clear, strictly watery rhinorrhea without any purulent characteristics. The patient reports intense, unremitting pruritus explicitly involving the nose and eyes. The bilateral, severely congested nasal passages have reliably forced constant obligate mouth breathing, leading to significant distress and inability to sleep.
Functional Status: Fatigued, unable to sleep due to nasal congestion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver concerned regarding potential asthma exacerbation or allergic systemic reaction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 25 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, frequent sneezing, obligate mouth breathing, no stridor, no audible wheeze.
Physical Exam: Nasal speculum examination visualizes pale, distinctly bluish, boggy, and severely swollen inferior nasal turbinates. The posterior pharyngeal wall displays a cobblestone appearance due to chronic lymphoid hyperplasia. There is bilateral conjunctival injection, severe reflex tearing, and significant ocular pruritus. Allergic shiners (darkened infraorbital venous pooling) and a transverse nasal crease are visible. Chest auscultation reveals clear air entry bilaterally with no wheeze or prolonged expiratory phase.
Environmental Observations: Pets present in the home, high pollen count day locally.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent antihistamines administered.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Allergic Rhinitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out due to the strict absence of generalized urticaria, wheeze, hypotension, or gastrointestinal symptoms.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out due to bilateral clear air entry, bilateral nasal congestion, and absence of sudden choking or stridor.

Viral Upper Respiratory Infection - Ruled out due to the absence of fever, the presence of intense pruritus, clear watery rhinorrhea, and pale/boggy (rather than erythematous) turbinates.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of paroxysmal sneezing and perceived respiratory difficulty required immediate after-hours assessment to confidently exclude life-threatening early anaphylaxis or acute asthma exacerbation. Objective clinical examination confirmed severe allergic rhinitis and ruled out lower respiratory tract involvement or systemic allergic compromise. Delaying this assessment until the next day carried an unacceptable risk of unrecognized anaphylaxis and condemned the patient to severe discomfort and sleep deprivation. Immediate administration of appropriate topical and systemic therapies resolved the perceived respiratory distress and secured a safe overnight plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Cetirizine 10mg stat. Prescribed intranasal corticosteroid (Mometasone 50mcg/spray) once daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: Administer the nasal spray while looking down toward the toes rather than tilting the head back. By immediately tasting less medication in the throat, the patient avoids the unpleasant bitter taste, reinforcing daily compliance with the preventative nasal spray.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss skin prick testing and long-term allergen avoidance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops lip or tongue swelling, difficulty breathing, an audible wheeze, generalized hives, or persistent vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptively localized allergic reaction; systemic allergic reactions can evolve unpredictably.
Clinical Course: Symptoms will significantly improve within 1-2 hours following the oral antihistamine. Nasal congestion will take several days of consistent intranasal steroid use to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if there is any swelling of the throat, voice changes, or difficulty drawing a breath.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The oral antihistamine blocks the immediate itching and sneezing. The nasal steroid spray requires daily use to decrease the swelling of the boggy tissues inside the nose.
Actionable Steps: Shower before bed to wash pollen off hair and skin. Keep bedroom windows closed during high pollen seasons.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Severe allergic rhinitis. No signs of anaphylaxis. Start daily antihistamine and nasal spray. See GP in 2 weeks.

SMS EDUCATION

